      "shareMode": "shareNow",
      "status": "sent",
      "text": "Remember last week's discussion about Microsoft and their \"principled stand\" on open weights for American competitiveness? https://www.linkedin.com/posts/satyanadella_open-weights-and-american-ai-leadership-activity-7486411389939392514--paP?utm_source=share&utm_medium=member_ios&rcm=ACoAAASrT0sBvdQnYhIMyN_5UEWSh_IliJaQrZU\nToday's announcement regarding Kimi K3 from Beijing-based Moonshot AI proves my point: The philosophy is secondary to the Azure meter.\nHere is a factual analysis of the announcement that Microsoft isn't saying out loud:\n1. \"Open\" vs. \"Geopolitically Restricted Closed\"\nLast week, Microsoft backed the argument that open weights matter for American competitiveness. This week, they are championing Kimi K3, a model from a Beijing-based lab (Moonshot AI), on Microsoft Foundry. Furthermore, Kimi K3 is not truly open. The license mandates that any company making over $20M in revenue using the model must negotiate a separate agreement with Moonshot. This isn't open; it\u2019s a gatekeeper model with commercial traps.\n2. The Performance vs. Control Paradox\nThe specs on Kimi K3 are undeniable: 2.8T MoE parameters, 1M token window, and it beats GPT-5.6 (the OpenAI crown jewel Microsoft uses in M365) on several frontier coding and document benchmarks.\nSo, the best performing model on Azure Foundry is now Chinese-developed and restricted. Why would Microsoft push this?\n3. The Inconvenient Truth: It's Just About Azure Compute\nThis isn't about fostering true open innovation; it's about Azure consumption. Kimi K3 costs $16.50 per million output tokens on Azure. Microsoft is perfectly happy to host a restricted, geopolitically complicated model, because every token generated bills Azure compute. The narrative of \"American competitiveness\" has been replaced by the pursuit of consumption revenue, regardless of where the weight comes from.\nLast week\u2019s tension has resolved: The flag Microsoft is planting isn't in the ground; it's on a toll booth.\n\nhttps://www.neowin.net/news/kimi-k3-model-is-now-deployable-on-microsoft-foundry-through-fireworks-ai/",
      "via": "buffer",
